# 🔭 Check Upcoming Home Assistant Changes Before Coding

Home Assistant announces API changes on its developer blog months before the old
way stops working. Write new code the new way right away, so it doesn't need a
refactor later.

## Before planning or writing code that uses an HA API

1. Read the developer blog posts of the last ~6 months that touch the APIs you
   use: <https://developers.home-assistant.io/blog/>. Typical areas: device and
   entity registry, `DeviceInfo`, entity base classes, config flow and entries,
   coordinator, services.
2. Check the symbol on the HA core `dev` branch
   (`homeassistant/helpers/*.py`) for `report_usage`, `deprecated` or
   `breaks_in_ha_version`.
3. Compare the HA version that introduced the new API with our minimum HA version
   (`requirements.txt`, `pyproject.toml`, `hacs.json`).
4. Name the checked posts in the plan.

## Decide

| Situation | Action |
| --- | --- |
| The new API exists in our minimum HA version | Use only the new API |
| The new API is newer than our minimum | Ask the user: raise the minimum or add a version check |
| The change is only announced for a future version | Keep the code easy to switch and tell the user |

- Never write new code with an API that is already deprecated.
- Existing code that uses a deprecated API: tell the user and propose a separate
  issue and PR. Ask before refactoring components, and don't mix it into an
  unrelated fix.
- A deprecation warning in the HA log (`Detected that custom integration
  'econet300' ...`) is a bug: open an issue with the removal version.

## Example

HA 2026.8 deprecated `DeviceInfo["via_device"]` in favour of `via_device_id`
(stops working in 2027.8). HA 2026.9 added child devices (`parent_device_id`) for
logical parts of one product. New device code uses `via_device_id` or child
devices, never `via_device`.
